{"id": 114735, "stem": "<div class=\"relative w-full min-w-0 max-w-full self-stretch\" id=\"question-text\"><span><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><mark class=\"bg-transparent dark:text-neutral-100\" data-markjs=\"true\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">A</mark> 54-year-old man comes to the office due to intermittent right lower extremity swelling.\u00a0 The patient also describes a feeling of heaviness and cramping in the affected leg.\u00a0 The symptoms occur most often in the evenings.\u00a0 Medical history includes hypertension treated with lisinopril.\u00a0 The patient drinks 1 or 2 alcoholic beverages on most days of the week and works as a store cashier.\u00a0 Temperature is 36.7 C (98.1 F), blood pressure is 120/76 mm Hg, pulse is 80/min, and respirations are 16/min.\u00a0 Jugular venous pressure is normal.\u00a0 The lungs are clear to auscultation.\u00a0 There are no murmurs.\u00a0 The abdomen is nondistended.\u00a0 Lower extremity examination shows edema of the right ankle.\u00a0 The left leg is normal.\u00a0 Scattered, superficial telangiectasias are present on the lower extremities.\u00a0 Doppler ultrasound examination of the right leg reveals no venous thrombosis.\u00a0 Which of the following is the most likely primary cause of this patient's edema?\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b</p></span></div>", "options": [{"label": "A", "text": "Decreased capillary oncotic pressure"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Decreased tone of the precapillary arterioles"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Increased capillary permeability"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure"}, {"label": "E", "text": "Increased renal sodium retention"}], "correctAnswer": "D", "explanation": "<div class=\"relative w-full min-w-0 max-w-full self-stretch\" id=\"question-explanation\"><span class=\"\"><div id=\"div_t67525\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><div class=\"group/triggerable\" data-is-open=\"false\"><div class=\"group/draggable\" data-is-draggable=\"true\" data-is-dragging=\"false\"><div class=\"group/resizable relative\" data-is-maximized=\"false\" data-is-resizable=\"true\" data-is-resizing=\"false\"><div class=\"relative max-w-full\"><div aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"pointer-events-none absolute left-0 top-0 bottom-0 w-8 z-10 transition-opacity duration-200 bg-gradient-to-r from-white dark:from-nbme-primary-dark-800 warm:from-egg-sour-50 to-transparent opacity-0\"></div><div aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"pointer-events-none absolute right-0 top-0 bottom-0 w-8 z-10 transition-opacity duration-200 bg-gradient-to-l from-white dark:from-nbme-primary-dark-800 warm:from-egg-sour-50 to-transparent opacity-0\"></div><div class=\"max-w-full overflow-x-auto [&amp;_td]:min-w-[8rem] [&amp;_th]:min-w-[8rem]\"><table class=\"border bg-transparent dark:text-white border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white hover:bg-transparent warm:hover:bg-transparent dark:hover:bg-transparent cursor-default mt-2 mb-3\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><tbody style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" colspan=\"3\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><mark class=\"bg-transparent dark:text-neutral-50\" data-markjs=\"true\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">E</mark>dema</strong></p></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Mechanism</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Examples</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Characteristics</strong></p></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">\u2191</strong><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"> Capillary hydrostatic pressure</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Heart failure</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Glomerulonephritis, renal failure</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Venous obstruction/insufficiency</li></ul></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" rowspan=\"2\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Pitting</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Symmetric (eg, systemic conditions) or asymmetric (eg, DVT)</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Dependent</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">\u2193</strong><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"> Capillary oncotic pressure </strong>(hypoalbuminemia)</p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Protein loss (eg, nephrotic syndrome)</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Decreased protein synthesis (eg, cirrhosis, malnutrition)</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">\u2191</strong><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"> Capillary permeability</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Burns, sepsis</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Allergic reactions</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Hereditary angioedema</li></ul></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" rowspan=\"2\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Nonpitting (lymphedema may initially be pitting)</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Usually asymmetric</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Nondependent* or dependent**</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Lymphatic obstruction</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Malignancy</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Congenital lymphedema</li></ul></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" colspan=\"3\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">*Edema is nondependent with increased capillary permeability.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">**Edema is dependent with lymphatic obstruction.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">DVT</strong> = deep venous thrombosis.</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div></div></div></div><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">This patient has unilateral lower extremity swelling with associated heaviness and cramping that are most prominent in the evening (likely after prolonged standing while he is at work), which is consistent with\u00a0<strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">chronic venous insufficiency </strong>(CVI).</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">CVI develops due to <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">venous hypertension</strong>, which can have multiple contributing processes, including:</p><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Increased orthostatic pressure (eg, <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">prolonged standing</strong>)</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Venous stasis from decreased muscular compression (eg, sedentary lifestyle)</li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Obstruction of venous return (eg, obesity)</li></ul><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">Chronic venous hypertension causes venous dilation and venous valve dysfunction, allowing <a class=\"hint\" data-exhibit-id=\"exhibit-ctf8fd1prjtmhgxx8cyojivq\" href=\"#exhibit-exhibit-ctf8fd1prjtmhgxx8cyojivq\">retrograde venous flow</a>.\u00a0 Pooled venous blood <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">increases local hydrostatic pressure</strong> and causes intravascular fluid to extravasate into nearby tissues.\u00a0 The consequent <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">edema</strong> usually worsens with prolonged standing and improves with leg elevation.\u00a0 Pooled blood in the deep venous system can also be shunted to nearby superficial vessels, manifesting as <a class=\"hint\" data-exhibit-id=\"exhibit-kx7yjkdinju6faktm0sgjdp0\" href=\"#exhibit-exhibit-kx7yjkdinju6faktm0sgjdp0\">telangiectasias</a> or <a class=\"hint\" data-exhibit-id=\"exhibit-o4mle0zefh3jfwtp3br19v4l\" href=\"#exhibit-exhibit-o4mle0zefh3jfwtp3br19v4l\">varicose veins</a>.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">CVI classically causes bilateral symptoms, but <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">early</strong> in the course of the disease it <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">can present unilaterally</strong>.\u00a0 Another common and serious cause of unilateral lower extremity edema is deep vein thrombosis; however, this is unlikely in this patient with no thrombosis on Doppler ultrasonography.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choice A)</strong>\u00a0 Decreased capillary oncotic pressure occurs when osmotically active intravascular substances (eg, albumin) are diminished (eg, increased urinary protein loss in nephrotic syndrome).\u00a0 To restore the concentration gradient between blood and the interstitium, intracapillary fluid extravasates into the interstitium.\u00a0 This causes generalized (rather than unilateral) edema.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choice B)</strong>\u00a0 Decreased tone of the precapillary arterioles increases capillary blood flow, resulting in increased intracapillary hydrostatic pressure; this is the mechanism behind calcium channel blocker\u2013associated edema.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choice C)</strong>\u00a0 Increased capillary permeability allows fluid to leak into the interstitium, and is the cause of edema in inflammatory conditions (eg, cellulitis) and ACE inhibitor\u2013induced angioedema (classically affecting the face/lips/tongue).\u00a0 Although increased vascular permeability occurs in CVI, it is secondary to venous congestion; the primary precipitating event is increased local hydrostatic pressure.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choice E)</strong>\u00a0 Increased renal sodium (and water) retention, mediated by the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, is one of the mechanisms by which edema occurs in conditions like cirrhosis and heart failure.\u00a0 It would be expected to cause generalized (vs unilateral) edema.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Educational objective:</strong>Chronic venous insufficiency develops due to venous hypertension, which can lead to venous dilation and venous valve dysfunction, allowing retrograde venous flow.\u00a0 The subsequent pooling of venous blood increases local hydrostatic pressure, resulting in lower extremity edema that may be unilateral (eg, early disease course) or bilateral.\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b</p></span></div>", "educationalObjective": "Chronic venous insufficiency develops due to venous hypertension, which can lead to venous dilation and venous valve dysfunction, allowing retrograde venous flow.  The subsequent pooling of venous blood increases local hydrostatic pressure, resulting in lower extremity edema that may be unilateral (eg, early disease course) or bilateral.\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u20